Likutay Halachos &middot; Orach Chaim

Hilchos Chol HaMoed

This halacha, based on LM II:4, introduces a new framework for understanding Chol HaMoed: the two-stage tikkun of the Ratzon (Divine Will) and the rescue of fallen souls trapped by the wisdom of nature.

The mechanism works as follows. The Three R&rsquo;galim reveal the Ratzon &mdash; that everything is conducted solely by Hashem&rsquo;s will, not by natural law. Against this revelation, the Maitzach HaNachash (Forehead of the Serpent = root of naturalistic philosophy) roars. Tzedakah subdues it. But if the revelation of the Ratzon is not yet strong enough, the serpent can return and cast doubt. A second application of tzedakah is needed to fully strengthen the Ratzon.

The question arises: why not do it all at once? And the answer is stunning: because there are fallen souls &mdash; Jews who were seduced by naturalistic philosophy &mdash; who can only be rescued between the two stages of tikkun. Before the first subduing of the Maitzach HaNachash, one cannot reach them at all (they are too deeply embedded). After the Ratzon is fully strengthened, there is no longer a &ldquo;gap&rdquo; through which to access them. Only in the intermediate space &mdash; when the serpent has been weakened but not yet destroyed &mdash; can one descend to those trapped souls and bring them back.

This is Chol HaMoed: the space between the first Yom Tov (initial revelation of Ratzon) and the last Yom Tov (final strengthening). Davar ha&rsquo;avaid &mdash; the one permitted labor &mdash; is the rescue of these lost souls. Hashem divided the tikkun into two precisely because He &ldquo;devises plans so that none shall be cast away from Him.&rdquo;

&#10042; Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman &#10042;
